.TITLE "git"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Git is a version control system used to track changes in code.

It was created by __Linus Torvalds__ in __2005__.
You can think of it like a timeline for your project, where each change is saved.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
git status

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**git status** shows the current state of your project.

Here, __git__ is the tool you're using.
__status__ is the subcommand that checks changes.

This helps you see what files are modified before doing anything else.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
git add file.txt

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**git add** prepares a file to be saved.

Here, __git__ is the tool.
__add__ is the subcommand that includes the file.
file.txt is the file being added.

This moves the file into staging, which is like a waiting area before saving.

.DIV "EXAMPLE"
git commit -m "save work"

.DIV "EXPLANATION"
**git commit** saves the staged changes.

Here, __git__ is the tool.
__commit__ records the changes.
__-m__ is a flag used to add a message.

This creates a checkpoint you can come back to later.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
Git tracks changes and keeps your project history organized.
Always check your work with git status before committing.
